On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 09:48:17 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> In the course of its work, model might identify conversations that have
> outdated information. /process-email should also groom the list, and
> provide updates to any stale threads based on new information.

Posted as "[PATCH] claude: Update stale threads from process-email"
[1]. The list researcher gets a third job: report threads whose last
word is now wrong because a series was revised, applied, or withdrawn,
a decision was reversed, or trunk moved on. A new step after the reply
then sends each such thread one update. It only adds replies. Per
"Claude doesn't archive email" [2] it never archives or closes
anything, since messages are immutable and archiving is yours.

> It should
> only do so when the original thread is out of date with respect to new
> info. We don't want it to generate noise.

The step skips a thread that is merely related, one where a later
message already carries the news, and one where the change would not
alter what a reader does next. Each thread gets one update, however
many points changed. It also skips threads whose latest message is
still in the inbox, because replying would mark that message replied
without answering it.

> It should provide references
> and summary information in its new replies.

Each update quotes only the outdated lines, says what is true now in a
sentence or two, and cites what superseded them by full subject with
the Message-ID in a footnote, the same way this reply does.

